# Smurf Attack Simulation & ICMP Traffic Analysis

## Overview

This lab demonstrates a controlled Smurf-style ICMP traffic scenario in an isolated laboratory environment.

The objective was to generate abnormal ICMP traffic toward a broadcast address and analyze the resulting network traffic from a Blue Team perspective.

## Lab Environment

- Kali Linux
- Ubuntu Linux
- hping3
- Wireshark
- Port Mirroring / SPAN

## Attack Simulation

A controlled ICMP traffic scenario was generated using hping3 in an isolated laboratory environment.

## Traffic Capture

Network traffic was captured using Wireshark on the physical monitoring workstation through a mirrored network interface.

## PCAP Analysis

The captured PCAP was analyzed using Wireshark.

| Metric | Result |
|---|---:|
| Total Packets | 70,427 |
| IPv4 Packets | 70,425 |
| ICMP Packets | 70,416 |
| ICMP Type | Echo Request (Type 8) |
| Source IP | 10.33.8.172 |
| Destination IP | 10.33.8.255 |
| ICMP Burst Duration | ~3.03 seconds |
| Approximate ICMP Rate | ~23,241 packets/sec |

The captured traffic consisted primarily of ICMP Echo Requests directed toward the broadcast address.

## Observations

- Very high ICMP packet rate
- Large number of ICMP Echo Requests
- Broadcast destination
- Short-duration traffic burst
- Repeated ICMP traffic

## Spoofing Analysis

The attack simulation used a spoofed-source configuration.

However, the captured packets show `10.33.8.172` as the source address. Therefore, source IP spoofing cannot be independently confirmed from this PCAP alone.

## Blue Team Analysis

From a defensive perspective, the following indicators may help identify abnormal ICMP activity:

- Sudden increase in ICMP traffic
- Extremely high ICMP packet rate
- Large number of Echo Requests
- Traffic directed toward a broadcast address
- Unexpected ICMP traffic patterns

## Mitigation

Possible defensive measures include:

- Disable directed broadcasts where not required
- Apply anti-spoofing controls
- Monitor abnormal ICMP traffic
- Apply appropriate ICMP rate limiting
- Use IDS/IPS monitoring

## Lessons Learned

This lab provided practical experience with:

- ICMP traffic analysis
- Broadcast traffic
- Wireshark
- PCAP analysis
- Network monitoring
- Detection indicators
- Blue Team investigation

## Conclusion

This lab demonstrated how abnormal ICMP traffic can be generated and investigated in a controlled environment.

The captured PCAP provided evidence of a high-volume ICMP Echo Request burst directed toward a broadcast address.
